From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 30 Mar 94 18:48:36 EST
Subject: [#94-3-374] REF: Epistemological Boundaries

Geoff Dale asked for references on the subject of Epistemological Boundaries in
Aspectual Reality Space so I will post a list of books of interest.  I have not
read all these, some I have, and some I have just seen quotations from, synopses
of, reviews of, paraphrases from or cross-referenced in Bibliographies.  Since
explicative definitions and contexts have been given elsewhere, very briefly:

Objective Reality is "They" or non-self, Consensual Reality is "We" or others
interacting with self, and Subjective Reality is "I" or self.  They are aspects
of existence, the only existence there is (no Many-Worlds solipsism).  There are
epistemological boundaries between each aspect of existence, that is, absolute
limitations to certain knowledge between them.


Epistemological Boundaries between Subjective and Consensual Reality
---------------------------
* Searle, John  "The Rediscovery of the Mind"  (1993)
Detailed account of subjective intentional mental states, the Background of
consciousness and strong criticism of cognitive science's postulates about deep
unconscious states of mind.

* Barkow, L. & Cosmides, L. & Tooby, J.  "The Adapted Mind"  (1992)
Reasoning about social exchange.  Empirical.

* Revel, Jean-Francois  "The Flight From Truth: The Reign of Deceit in the Age
of Information"  (1991)
How consensual purpose is forged, to the detriment of both the truth and
self-determination.

* Gazzaniga, Michael  "The Social Brain: Discovering the Networks of the Mind"
(1985)
The intimate flow of concepts in both directions across the boundary to forge a
self within society.

* Axelrod, Robert  "The Evolution of Cooperation"  (1984)
Trust based on forging of consensual purpose.

* Berger & Luckmann  "The Social Construction of Reality"  (1967)
Knowledge is socially constructed, facts reflect purposes.


Epistemological Boundaries between Objective and Consensual Reality
---------------------------
* Brown, James Robert  "Smoke and Mirrors: How Science Reflects Reality"  (1993)
Realist epistemology.  Refutation of some major anti-science proponents (incl.
Rorty).

* Hull, D.  "Science as a Process"  (1988)
Evolutionary epistemology.

* Popper, Karl  "Objective Knowledge"  (1973)
Evolutionary epistemology.

* Whitehead, Alfred North  "Science and the Modern World"  (1925)
Puts the relation between the scientific observer and the subjectively
reflective individual in a rich historical perspective.  Also process philosophy
and evolutionary epistemologies are closely related.


Rationality - How we deal with the dynamic coupling between the aspects of
reality and the boundaries between them
---------------------------
* Gelernter, David  "The Muse in the Machine: Computerizing the Poetry of Human
Thought"  (1994)
Variations of cognitive processes.  High focus (analytical), medium focus
(emotion) & low focus (associative creativity).  Empirical.

* Nozick, Robert  "The Nature of Rationality"  (1993)
Good lists of checks for decision value formulation and heuristics for
problem-solving.  Major caveat on Symbolic Utility legitimately being
incorporated into rational decision theory.

* Meyer, Herbert  "Hard Thinking"
Argues that science, with all its insistence on verification, be adopted to the
art of political pronunciamento.  Bring objective facts closer to subjective
purpose within consensual reality.

* De Sousa, R.  "The Rationality of Emotion"  (1987)
Philosophical account of extraordinarily complex, multilevel nature of emotions.

* Holland, John & Holyoak, Keith & Nisbett, Richard & Thagard, Paul  "Induction:
Processes of Inference, Learning and Discovery"  (1986)
Highly recommended from leading AI researchers.

* Ryn, Claes  "Will, Imagination, and Reason"  (1986)
Cogent presentation of the complex interrelationships between subjective
selectional systems and consensual reality.

* Kahneman, Daniel & Slovic, Paul & Tversky, Amos, eds.  "Judgment Under
Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases"  (1982)
Uncertainty is a major, unavoidable epistemological limit.

* Toulmin, S.  "Human Understanding"  (1972)
Evolutionary epistemology.


Evolution - How the aspects have developed in the form they have through
historical processes and following biophysical laws
---------------------------
* Kauffman, Stuart  "The Origins of Order: Self Organization and Selection in
Evolution"  (1993)
In my opinion, this marriage of Spontaneous Order and Biological Selection is
every bit as much of a scientific shift in the way we view life as Darwin's
"Origin of Species" was.  Kauffman won't be right on everything but neither was
Darwin.  There is no going back to over-reliance on reductionism from here.
Excellent models on technological and economic takeoff.

* Edelman, Gerald  "Bright Air, Brilliant Fire: On the Matter of the Mind"
(1993)
How we know, why our minds work the way they do, and what the physical bases are
of thoughts and emotions.  The major disagreement I have with the Theory of
Neuronal Group Selection is that I do not think selection works at the group
level.  After reading Kauffman, I think all selection, whether biological or
memetic, occurs at the individual level around attractors which give it the
appearance of group selection.

* Alexander, R.D.  'Evolution of the Human Psyche' in "The Human Revolution"
eds. P. Mellars and C. Stringer  (1989)
Self-deceit as an adaptive phenomenon in the deceiving of others during the
struggle for survival.

* Boyd, R. & Richerson, P.J.  "Culture and the Evolutionary Process"  (1985)
A balanced account of how human social behavior and evolution may interact.

* Mayr, E.  "The Growth of Biological Thought: Diversity, Evolution, and
Inheritance"  (1982)
One of the best accounts of Darwin, Darwinism, and the "subtheories" that make
up the modern theory of evolution.

* Bergson, Henri  "Creative Mind"  (1946)
Proponent of the elan vital.  As you read this, substitute Spontaneous Order for
elan vital to eliminate vitalism's errors of straying from physicalism.  This is
the major forerunner of the concept of negentropy and syntropy, highly
recommended for the historical context of extropy.  Very good on the
relationship of subjective purpose to objective truth.  


