From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 30 Mar 94 19:00:26 EST
Subject: [#94-3-375] PCR: The Voice of Reason

Max More wrote 3/28/94: <Such a contextual certainty involves being thoroughly
*convinced* of a belief, but does not imply that the belief is held
dogmatically, that it is held to be beyond criticism, beyond revision in
principle.>

This helps clarify the use of "certainty" in such a way that we can avoid using
phrases like "less certain."  I might point out that when contextual certainty
is achieved, the "belief" becomes "knowledge."

Max again: <Finally, holding everything open to criticism does not means you
hold that there are no true statements or valid arguments, or that for every
proposition there exists a successful criticism of it. Such a relativistic view
would be precisely what pancritical rationalism is intended to avoid.
Relativism and the problem of ultimate commitments are closely tied to one
another, and PCR provides an effective response to both.>

Whew!  This needed to be stated explicitly.  Relativism is bad.

Max again: <The preeminent logician and philosopher of language, W.V. Quine, in
his "Two Dogmas of Empiricism" has argued that we can always 
maintain a belief, no matter how bizarre, so long as we are willing to make
changes at other points in our web of belief.>

I agree with Quine's coherency epistemology with the modification that the
coherent web of beliefs coheres around the various subjective purposes an
individual has.  It is a complicated picture and can be chock full of
contradictions if purposes are in conflict.  The grounding of rationality is on
these purposes and the values constructed around them.

Max again: <PCR (and all other beliefs of a good PCRist) is grounded in 
its so far having  survived criticism.  This nonjustificational sense of 
"grounding" would be  acceptable to PCR.>

I don't see this as "grounding" because it has no anchor point other than
relying on the strength of intellect of those applying criticism to one's
beliefs.  This is the maieutic method of Socrates, seek out the strongest
intellects for constant criticism of all positions without any acknowledgement
of other grounding of rationality.  This method led directly to the Cynics, the
Epicureans, and the Stoics which then led to Christianity in search of some
ground.

Max again: <PCR *is* grounded in subjective purposes in the sense  that, if my
one of my goals was not to be as rational as possible, I would not be
interested in PCR.  Even if PCR can be successfully defended against all
criticisms, it will never appeal to persons who care nothing for consistency,
truth, or reason.  In this sense, PCR is grounded in one's values and so is not
"smug nihilism".  Note that this is not to say that PCR is *justified* in terms
of  one's values; it means only that one's acceptance of PCR is explained by
one's  valuing rationality in combination with believing that PCR, so far,
provides the  best, most consistent, characterization of what it means to be a
rationalist.>

I support this unreservedly.  I am glad to see it made explicit because only by
making it explicit does PCR avoid being merely smug nihilism.

Max again: <After all this discussion of PCR, I'm disappointed that you wrote
the above.  Surely you can see that your characterization grossly misrepresents
PCR.  I really don't understand how you can think that PCR  leads to
"conventional authority worship" when it clearly does the opposite.>

Since you have grounded PCR in subjective purpose explicitly, you can ignore my
criticism completely.  It was predicated on no one having done this before.

Max again: <I very much want to learn more about Edelman's and Kauffman's work.
In fact I welcome and even urge you to write a book review for EXTROPY,  and/or
a concise but reasonably comprehensive summary or this list (or the ExI- Essay
List).>

I will reevaluate what I've done so far, and see what I can come up with.

Max again: <For example,  experiments have demonstrated that first-person
reports of cognitive processes  of people with eidetic "photographic" memory are
in error.  The subjects believe  that they are looking at an internal image in
just the way that you'd look at a  photograph, but cleverly designed experiments
have shown that they are really  scanning through information sequentially (just
much faster than the rest of us),  and *not* moving an inner eye across an
internal image.>

I believe that this is how "intuition" works also.  It is simply very fast
associative connections being made, generally after long cogitation in low focus
thinking.  You might take a look at David Gelernter's (author of "Mirror
Worlds") new book "The Muse in the Machine: Computerizing the Poetry of Human
Thought"  (1994).  He looks at cognition based on his experimental results with
massive parallel processing induction (his "plunge-squish" method if you
recall).  He describes cognitive states of high focus (analytical), medium focus
(emotion) and low focus (associative creativity).  The experimental results you
reference simply show that science doesn't deal with metaphor very well since
it's a qualitative subjective process and not a quantitative objective process.
The experiments verify that the way people describe what is happening in their
subjective mental states does not correspond to a reductionist's explanation of
it.  But as metaphor, which is completely valid cognition, there is no error to
either descriptions of eidetic memory or intuition.

Max again: <WE *can* be mistaken about our own mental events.  We may act on the
basis of  desires that we claim not to have.  We may mistake the causes of our
judgments  and choices and incorrectly identify the influences on our behavior*.
A dream  pain feels real during the dream, but upon awakening we realize that it
isn't.   Sensations of extreme cold can be mistaken for extreme heat...> and <In
particular, how does Edelman's and Kauffman's writings speak against the
corrigibility of our knowledge of our own mental processes?  The incorrigibility
view -- very Cartesian and not what I'd expect to hear from you -- seems to be
made problematic not just by theory but by experiment.>

We must be very careful to distinguish between what is happening in subjective
reality ("I") and what is happening in consensual reality ("We") here.  In
subjective reality, there is no distinction between appearance and reality.
Dream pains that feel real during a dream are indeed real.  How could they be
otherwise?  When we awaken we do not realize that it wasn't "real", we realize
that it was subjective and without direct external cause (although obviously it
had indirect external cause that formed our memories of what pain is).
Remembered pain is very real pain.

I am going to quote some Searle from "The Rediscovery of the Mind" about the
epistemological boundary between subjective and consensual reality and why
errors about subjective mental states only exist when we attempt to bring them
into consensual reality.  "The facts are that biological processes produce
conscious mental phenomena, and these are irreducibly subjective.  But just as
the metaphor of introspection broke down when the only thing to be observed was
the observing itself, so the metaphor of a private inner space breaks down when
we understand that there isn't anything like a space into which I can enter,
because I cannot make the necessary distinctions between the three elements of
myself, the act of entering, and the space in which I am supposed to enter.
Though I can easily observe another person, I cannot observe his or her
subjectivity.  And worse yet, I cannot observe  my own subjectivity, for any
observation that I might care to make is itself that which was supposed to be
observed."
	This is what Kauffman describes as a holistic "knower-and-known" system.
It is only when we try to describe our mental states to others, that errors
arise, either through the inadequacy of language to describe such rich mental
states or through confusion about what is really going on in our mental states.
Our actual mental states themselves are true.  They are objective truth to
outsiders, every bit as much as facts in objective reality are.  They are simply
inaccessible to outsiders because of the epistemological boundary that makes us
unique.  To quote from Edelman:  "Qualia, individual to each of us, are
recategorizations by higher-order consciousness of value-laden perceptual
relations in each sensory modality or their conceptual combinations with each
other.  Once a self is developed through social and linguistic interactions on a
base of primary consciousness, a world is developed that requires naming and
intending.  This world reflects inner events that are recalled, and imagined
events, as well as outside events that are perceptually experienced.  Embodiment
imposes ineluctable limits.  Scientific study recognizes this limit without
indulging in mystical exercises or illusions.  The reason for the limit is
straightforward:  The forms of embodiment that lead to consciousness are unique
in each individual, unique to his or her body and individual history."
	Confusions about our mental states that cause errors in communication
within consensual reality can arise from:  self-deception (willfully redirecting
mental states that conflict with subjective purposes brought to the forefront
amongst all such purposes at that particular moment), misinterpretation
(difficulty in providing the proper context of a given mental state within
overall consciousness), and inattention (low focus of thought rather than highly
focused analytical cognitive mode).  The mental states themselves are not in
error, the ability to describe them to others is the epistemological boundary.
Nor does any "deep unconscious" intentional state exist that is inaccessible to
consciousness.  Most psychofunctionalist theories of mind want to posit the
existence of such states so that causal relations between all mental states can
be established.  After absorbing Kauffman's ideas of the role of complexity and
attractors within "knower-and-known" holistic systems, I take the position that
these causal relations cannot be specified logically, only statistically within
ensembles of neuronal maps.  Random determination rather than direct, in-line,
causal determination that functionalism thrives on.

